# Engineering workflows

## Planning and decomposition

For substantial work, state the outcome, acceptance criteria, risks,
dependencies, and explicit exclusions. Split the work into dependency-ordered
vertical slices, each with one observable behavior and a verification command.
Do not begin a later slice while the current slice is failing or unexplained.

## Bug diagnosis

Do not patch a symptom first. Reproduce the defect, minimize the input or call
sequence, capture expected versus actual behavior, form a testable hypothesis,
and run the smallest experiment that distinguishes it. Add a regression test,
fix the root cause, then verify focused and affected-scope behavior.

## Domain modeling

Use the terminology in `CONTEXT.md` and nearby code. Before adding complex
logic, identify the domain concepts, invariants, lifecycle, and boundary
representations. Prefer names that match the protocol vocabulary. If
terminology or invariants are genuinely ambiguous, ask instead of inventing
parallel concepts; record durable decisions in `CONTEXT.md` or an ADR.

## Review

Before completion, review the complete diff as a separate engineer. Check
observable correctness, tests and negative cases, compatibility, error
handling, security invariants, dependency direction, naming, coupling,
complexity, generated outputs, and accidental scope. Prefer concrete defects
over stylistic preferences, and do not claim a check passed unless it ran.
